An :ERROR handler placed first in its procedure, after only :PARAMETERS
and :DECLARE (which do not count as protected statements). It protects
nothing and the code after it never runs on the success path:
error_handler_structure "handler placed first" (diag.error_handler_structure
third Flags fence).
-- input.ssl --
:PROCEDURE ComputeYield;
	:PARAMETERS nInput, nOutput;
	:DECLARE nYield;
	:ERROR;
	nYield := nOutput / nInput;
	:RETURN nYield;
:ENDPROC;
-- format --
:PROCEDURE ComputeYield;
	:PARAMETERS nInput, nOutput;
	:DECLARE nYield;

	:ERROR;
		nYield := nOutput / nInput;

		:RETURN nYield;
:ENDPROC;
-- diagnostics --
4:2-4:8 warning deprecated_keyword: ':ERROR' is legacy error handling. Prefer ':TRY' / ':CATCH' / ':FINALLY'
4:2-4:8 error error_handler_structure: ':ERROR' protects only the statements before it in the procedure; placed first it protects nothing. Move it to the end of the procedure
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
4:2-4:8 warning deprecated_keyword: ':ERROR' is legacy error handling. Prefer ':TRY' / ':CATCH' / ':FINALLY'
4:2-4:8 error error_handler_structure: ':ERROR' protects only the statements before it in the procedure; placed first it protects nothing. Move it to the end of the procedure
